1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: Four percent. So almost all of it went to waste.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: Almost all. Then a travelling salesman in Germany, Nicolaus Otto, learned of it, and started building engines himself.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: In 1862 he tried one that compressed its charge first. It ran for a few minutes, then destroyed itself.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: That same year, in France, Alphonse Beau de Rochas patented the four-stroke cycle. But he never built an engine.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: So the idea existed on paper before anyone made it run.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: Meanwhile, in eighteen sixty-four, Otto and Eugen Langen founded the first company devoted to making engines.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: Their atmospheric engine won a gold medal at the 1867 Paris World's Fair, at about 12 percent efficiency.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: That's three times Lenoir's.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: Then, in 1876, Otto's company built an engine that compressed its charge in four strokes.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: It was an immediate success, and the first commercially successful engine to compress its charge in the cylinder.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: And two of Otto's former engineers, Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach, made it small and fast enough for vehicles.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: But in eighteen eighty-six the patent was cancelled in Germany, because Beau de Rochas had patented the cycle first.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: So a patent for an engine nobody had built ended Otto's monopoly.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: And neither Otto nor Daimler had even known that patent existed.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERB: Still, the engines spread. By 1889, more than 50 companies were building Otto-type engines.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: And the cycle still carries Otto's name. It's called the Otto cycle.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERB: So why does squeezing first help so much? Engineers answer with a graph of pressure against volume.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: This is the idealised version, the Otto cycle, traced as our engine turns.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: During intake, the volume grows, at about the pressure outside.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: During compression, the volume shrinks tenfold, and the pressure climbs steeply.
